<title>CANADA: Canadian foreign reserves fall in July.</title>
<headline>Canadian foreign reserves fall in July.</headline>
<dateline>OTTAWA 1997-08-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Canadian foreign reserve holdings fell in July, the Finance Department said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>   RESERVES			  July 97     June 97</p>
<p>   Total			   19.553 bln  21.258 bln</p>
<p>   U.S. dollars		  16.678 bln  18.338 bln</p>
<p>   Other foreign currencies     507 million     502 million</p>
<p>   Gold				 147 million     150 million</p>
<p>   Special drawing rights     1.118 bln   1.142 bln</p>
<p>   IMF reserves		   1.103 bln   1.126 bln</p>
<p>Note - Figures in U.S. dollars.  </p>
<p>Official government operations decreased reserves by US$116 million.</p>
<p>Details of the changes in the month:</p>
<p>    Gain on gold sales		   n/a</p>
<p>    Earnings on investments	    +92</p>
<p>    Revaluation effects		  -64</p>
<p>    Foreign currency debt	   -1,636</p>
<p>    Other transactions		   +19</p>
<p>NOTE - in millions of U.S. dollars  </p>
<p>During July, Canada Bills decreased by $1.135 billion to a level of outstanding bills of $4.757 billion and Canada Notes increased by $5.0 million to a level of outstanding notes of $1.297 billion.</p>
<p>The Canada Euro Medium-Term Note program commenced in July with sales of $493.9 million. A $2.0 billion bond was repaid on July 7. A five-year global bond was issued to raise $1.0 billion, it said.</p>
<p>((Ottawa Bureau (613) 235-6745))</p>
